## Where the prefill speedup comes from

Over the 5 nodes with all four stages measured, excluding the two repack-gate
failures:

| step | 27B gemm | 27B gemv (decode) |
|---|---:|---:|
| upstream to +repack | **6.25x** | 2.24x |
| +repack to +prefetch | **1.05x** | 1.58x |
| +prefetch to +vd4dots | **2.35x** | 3.54x |
| **total (geo mean)** | **15.47x** | 12.64x |
| aggregate over those nodes | **17.82x** | |

This reproduces the 4B prefill result (6.11x / 1.04x / 2.46x = 15.64x) on a
model 6.5x larger with a different architecture. Prefill takes its win from the
**layout**; decode takes its win from **prefetch and the dot instruction**.

The absolute cycles make the point without any ratio. On `ffn_gate` at the
`+repack` stage:

| path | tokens | cycles | per token |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| decode | 2 | 73,356,044 | 36,678,022 |
| prefill | 4 | **45,434,691** | **11,358,673** |

Prefill does twice the tokens in 62% of the cycles -- 3.2x cheaper per token --
because the gemm loads each weight vector once and fires four dots against it
(`arch/riscv/repack.cpp:1967-1972`), while the gemv reloads it per token.

## Notes

- **`ssm_alpha` and `ssm_beta` get nothing on prefill either.** All four builds
  land within 0.3% (936,969 / 939,289 / 939,798 / 938,688). 48 rows fails the
  repack gate `ne1 % 64 == 0`, so every build runs the identical stock kernel.
  They are 0.06% of the layer's weights but **1.4% of its optimized cycles** --
  the same over-weight dead spot decode has. Padding them to 64 rows is the
  cheapest win available on this model.
- **Total speedup tracks node size, as on 4B.** `attn_v`/`attn_k` (1024 rows)
  reach 11.40x while `attn_gate`/`ssm_out`/`attn_output` reach 18.9-19.1x. On
  the small nodes prefill gains **no more than decode** (11.40x vs 11.24x),
  because the per-call scalar activation quantize dominates when there are few
  row tiles to amortize it over. See `profiling_gemm.md` for the decomposition.
- **Upstream is exactly linear in tokens**, confirmed on every node measured so
  far -- 1.97x to 2.01x for 2x the tokens (K=49 1.98x, K=50 1.99x, K=51 2.01x,
  K=52 1.97x, K=57 2.00x, K=59 1.98x). `.nrows = 1` (`ggml-cpu.c:257`) means one
  `vec_dot` per (weight row, token); there is no prefill kernel upstream.
- **Prefetch is worth slightly more here than on 4B** (1.05-1.08x vs
  1.03-1.05x). 27B tensors are far larger than the 2 MB L3, so more miss
  latency survives the M-tile reuse.
- **`ssm_out` and `attn_output` are the same shape** (6144 x 5120) and agree to
  2.4% at upstream, matching the 2.6% decode spread `profiling_data.md`
  reports. At `d4` they are 2.5% apart, wider than the 0.04% decode figure.

## Still running

Five `pristine` legs -- K=48, 53, 54, 55, 56 -- are the most expensive in the
grid (183M to 381M cycles each, hours of RTL apiece). Their `+repack`,
`+prefetch` and `+vd4dots` columns are complete; only the `total` column waits
on them. If they prove impractical, upstream can be derived as 2x the decode
value, which the linearity check above validates to within 3% on this model.
